<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bareilly</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bareilly is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the Ramganga River, and the main city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a> region. It became the seat of the Rohilla chiefs under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafiz-rahmat-khan">Hafiz Rahmat Khan</a> in the mid-18th century. After passing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> in 1774, it was ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in 1801 and was a centre of the revolt of 1857.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bareilly.jpg" alt="Bareilly" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wp masifnoor · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dargah_Aala_Hazrat_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bareilly lies in the Indo-Gangetic Plains on the bank of the Ramganga River, about 252 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> and 265 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>. The plain is level and well watered, with fertile alluvial soil, although the lower plain is flood-prone. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, the region, called Panchala, was the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draupadi">Draupadi</a>, and folklore says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Gautama Buddha</a> visited the ancient fortress city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahichchhatra">Ahichchhatra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest settlement was founded in 1537 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> chieftain Jagat Singh Katehriya, who named it after his two sons Bansaldev and Baraldev. The town came under Mughal rule in 1569, and in 1657 the Mughal governor Mukrand Rai laid the foundation of the modern city. As Mughal power weakened, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a> rose, with Bareilly as a major centre. Under Hafiz Rahmat Khan Barech, Rohilkhand blocked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> expansion into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> in 1761.</p><p>In 1774 the combined forces of Nawab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafiz-rahmat-khan">Hafiz Rahmat Khan</a>, who was killed in battle, ending Rohilla rule. The Nawab of Awadh surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a> to the Company in a treaty of 10 November 1801. A military station was established south of the city in 1811, and a fort was built in 1816. Bareilly was freed by rebels during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a> and remained independent under Khan Bahadur Khan until the British re-annexed it in 1858.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bareilly is a city in the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> and the centre of the historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a>. It is the place of origin of the Barelvi movement, a Sunni Islamic movement formed by the scholar Ahmad Raza Khan to counter the growing influence of the Deobandi movement; his shrine at the Bareilly Dargah is visited by millions of followers each year. The city has been known as Nath Nagri because of its ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temples, and is a centre of furniture manufacturing and of trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, cereal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bareilly” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Ali Muhammad's death in 1748, Hafiz Rahmat Khan ruled as regent for his sons and led a confederacy of Rohilla chiefs from <b>Bareilly</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/rohilla-state">Rohilla State</a></li></ul></section>
